the Xbox one had a rocky start when it  first launched in 2013  since then Microsoft has ditched the  Kinect made several software  improvements on the Xbox one s  represents the company's latest attempt  at bolstering the Xbox ecosystem with a  slimmer more capable design but in 2016  is it enough  let's find out the Xbox one s comes in  three variants there's a $300 500  gigabyte model the $350 one terabyte  model and the $400 2 terabyte model  which I'm reviewing here it doesn't come  with a Kinect but if you already have  one with the older model Microsoft says  you're eligible for a free USB adapter  otherwise it will cost you 40 bucks the  original Xbox one was bulky and frankly  looked a little bit like a VCR this  version here is 40 percent smaller and  uses a mostly white aesthetic overall it  looks clean  despite its smaller size it never got  loud or hot either  perhaps the most impressive thing here  is that Microsoft managed to squeeze the  power break into the chassis while  making it smaller  considering the Xbox one's original  power brick was massive that's a  technical marvel in my book with the  exception of the missing Kinect port the  S maintains the same ports the new  chassis also rearranges certain buttons  for instance the controller pairing  button and single USB port that used to  be located on the left of the console  have been moved to the front where they  are more easily accessible the  capacitive touch button has also been  replaced with the physical one which is  good for preventing accidental shutdowns  the system's cooling has also been  redesigned to allow you to position it  to stand vertically with the consoles  included stand inside the Xbox the main  ingredients remain largely the same it  still uses an 8 core CPU from AMD with 8  gigabytes of ddr3 RAM the GPU frequency  has received a 7 percent boost going  from 853 megahertz to 914 you likely  won't notice improved  graphical performance but the extra  performance Headroom allows the console  to support HDR and 4k video streaming  what is HDR it stands for high dynamic  range and it provides a much higher  contrast ratio between black and whites  it also displays a much wider visible  color gamut essentially like 4k it's  poised to be the next  and panel technology like 4k you'll need  a TV that supports it games will also  need to support it  Gears of War 4 and Forza horizon 3 are  examples of upcoming HDR compatible  games one disappointing aspect of the  Xbox 1s is that it still uses a slow  5400 rpm hard drive  sadly you still can't upgrade the drive  for a better speed or more storage  moving on to the controller it remains  largely the same the biggest difference  here is that it features a new textured  grip which gives it a gentle almost  sandpapery feel Microsoft also says the  bump sticks have been redesigned to  withstand more wear and tear but I  couldn't feel the difference with  Microsoft announcing that its new  project Scorpio is on the way next year  that leaves the Xbox one s in a weird  spot who exactly is this for well if you  don't already have an Xbox one and one  an HDR 4k blu-ray player then this  console is a must buy as far as I'm  concerned it's even a little cheaper  than standalone players if you already  own an Xbox one  I wouldn't upgrade unless you really  want the 4k HDR support if you've been  on the fence with the Xbox one have been  waiting for the library to mature the  Xbox 1's makes it a great time to jump  on board its slim is the only model that  can be outfitted with a 2 terabyte hard  drive is priced fairly and offers the  effort mentioned of video features if  you don't care about all that and just  want to play xbox one games I'd actually  recommend the original Xbox one over the  ass it's 500 gigabytes key retails for  $250 which is $50 less than the  comparable s model the Xbox one s might  not be a huge overhaul you'll want to  wait for project Scorpio if that's what  you seek but it is without a doubt the  sleekest most feature-rich Xbox yet
 

